const methodItems = [
  {
    icon: 'target' as const,
    title: 'Dry dust comes off first',
    description:
      'Everything gets dusted or vacuumed with a brush head before anything damp appears. Dust plus a wet cloth is mud, and on a white slat or a fan blade that means smears you then have to chase around the room.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'idea' as const,
    title: 'Ceiling fans, blade by blade',
    description:
      'The felt of dust sits on the top of each blade where nobody sees it, and the housing and pull chain collect it too. Each blade is supported and wiped rather than swiped at, because bending a blade is what puts a wobble in a fan.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'spark' as const,
    title: 'Hard blinds and shutters, slat by slat',
    description:
      'Aluminium, timber and faux timber slats get done individually, both faces, along with the head rail, the cords or wand and the bottom rail. There is no fast way to do this properly, which is the entire reason people book it out.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'bloom' as const,
    title: 'Fabric handled dry, or not at all',
    description:
      // ⚠️ LAUNCH BLOCKER. Confirm whether TLB cleans fabric blinds.
      'Roller, roman and sunscreen blinds mark permanently if they are treated like a hard surface, and a water ring on a fabric blind is worse than the dust was. These get dry methods and spot work only. [Confirm whether TLB cleans fabric blinds at all.]',
  },
  {
    icon: 'puzzle' as const,
    title: 'Tracks, sills and pelmets',
    description:
      'The window track is where the dust ends up once the blind above it has been done, along with dead insects and sand, so it gets cleared and wiped as part of the same job rather than left as the giveaway.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'house' as const,
    title: 'Only as high as is safe',
    description:
      // ⚠️ LAUNCH BLOCKER. Confirm the actual limit. See the file header.
      'Standard ceilings and windows are routine. A raked ceiling, a stairwell void or a second storey reached from outside is a different job with different equipment, and we will tell you when something is beyond a step ladder rather than improvise. [Confirm TLB’s height limit.]',
  },
];

// The one genuinely useful warning on the page, and it is a real one: the
// ultrasonic question decides whether the customer loses their windows for a
// day. Callout rather than TextBlock because it is a short aside.
const scopeCallout = {
  heading: 'Ask whether your blinds are leaving the house',
  body: [
    'There are two ways to clean a blind, and they are not the same service. In situ means it stays on the window and is cleaned in place, which is quicker and cheaper and suits most dusty but otherwise sound blinds. Ultrasonic means the blind is taken down, put through a bath off site and rehung a day or two later, which gets a far better result on something greasy or genuinely neglected, and leaves you with a bare window in the meantime. Neither is better in general. Just make sure you know which one you are buying.',
  ],
};

const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Most damaged blinds in this region are not damaged by dirt. They are damaged by someone cleaning them, and the two ways it happens are worth knowing before you spend a Saturday on a ladder. The first is water: a fabric roller or roman blind that gets wiped with a damp cloth will very often dry with a visible tidemark, and unlike a carpet there is no way to blend it out afterwards. The second is force. A venetian slat wiped hard without being supported from behind bends, and a bent slat never sits flat again, so the blind will not close properly from then on.',
  'Ceiling fans have their own version of this. A fan blade is a balanced aerofoil, and pushing down hard on the end of one while you scrub the top of it is how a fan that ran silently for ten years starts to wobble. If you are doing it yourself, hold the blade from underneath and wipe with the other hand. If a fan already wobbles, that is an electrician or a balancing kit rather than a cleaner.',
];

const clusterServices = otherApplianceServices(OWN_HREF);

